&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Senior Site Due Diligence Design Engineer&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;About the Team&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The Senior Site Due Diligence Design Engineer plays a critical role in CoreWeave&#39;s data centre development pipeline by providing the first full technical evaluation of prospective sites. As the first engineering representative in site selection and due diligence, you will bring a broad, multidisciplinary perspective to judge how well each site can support CoreWeave&#39;s requirements across all major design disciplines.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;You will be the technical bridge between the Site Selection team and the broader Design &amp;amp; Engineering organisation. From first site introduction through lease execution or acquisition, you will lead technical due diligence. That includes evaluating infrastructure capacity, utility availability, building suitability, land use and entitlements, and early design constraints, and working directly with data centre providers, utilities, landlords, and external partners. Once a site is secured, you will hand it off to the assigned Design Manager for full project design. The role calls for strong engineering judgment, the ability to interpret information across disciplines, and the confidence to represent CoreWeave&#39;s technical priorities in early strategic conversations.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This role requires frequent travel to prospective data centre locations across the EMEA and APAC regions.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;Key Responsibilities:&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Initial Technical Evaluation:&lt;/strong&gt; Act as the first technical evaluator for prospective data centre sites across electrical, mechanical, architectural, civil, and IT/LV disciplines, identifying technical risks, constraints, opportunities, and required upgrades.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Early-Phase Design Leadership:&lt;/strong&gt; Lead the design team from initial site concept through lease execution or acquisition, coordinating all design activities in these early phases. Take part in early design and scoping discussions to align everyone on CoreWeave&#39;s requirements and technical expectations.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Proposal &amp;amp; Document Review:&lt;/strong&gt; Review provider proposals, capacity offers, utility information, conceptual layouts, and as-built drawings to confirm they align with CoreWeave&#39;s design principles. Make sure technical requirements are correctly reflected in early documents such as LOIs, provider proposals, and preliminary agreements.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;External Engagement:&lt;/strong&gt; Work directly with landlords, developers, data centre providers, and utilities during early due diligence. Run early technical workshops with providers so they correctly understand CoreWeave&#39;s design expectations and project objectives.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Power &amp;amp; Utility Assessment:&lt;/strong&gt; Review site infrastructure, including power availability, redundancy options, upstream utility constraints, delivery timelines, and required upgrades.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Mechanical Feasibility:&lt;/strong&gt; Assess heat rejection options, water availability and quality, mechanical yard capacity, and compatibility with CoreWeave cooling strategies (e.g., chilled water plants, CDUs, TCS loops).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Building Suitability:&lt;/strong&gt; Evaluate structural capacity, floor loading, egress paths, ceiling heights, roof strength, envelope condition, and potential for expansion.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Land Use &amp;amp; Entitlements:&lt;/strong&gt; Evaluate land use, zoning, permitting, and entitlement feasibility at a high level, and identify regulatory constraints or municipal approval risks that could affect schedule, density, or constructability.&lt;/li&gt;
